IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `dog-act-domain`

-- =============================================================================
-- Dog Act 1976 (Western Australia) -- Part VII, Enforcement (ss 43-46A)
--
-- Isomorphic encoding of the whole of Part VII ("Enforcement") of the Dog
-- Act 1976 (WA) (No. 58 of 1976): s. 43 (offences relating to enforcement
-- etc.), s. 43AA (false or misleading information), s. 43A (name and
-- address to be supplied), s. 43B (general powers of relevant persons),
-- s. 44 (enforcement proceedings), s. 45 (evidentiary provisions), s. 45A
-- (modified penalties) and s. 46A (dog training course / ownership ban
-- orders), as at the compilation dated 28 Nov 2024 [version 08-b0-00],
-- re-verified current 2026-09-09. Source: lines 1531-1642 of the verbatim
-- compilation (mrdoc_47983.txt, this Act's source bundle) -- confirmed to
-- be exactly the span of "Part VII -- Enforcement" (line 1491 to the line
-- immediately before "Part VIII -- Civil remedies, etc." at line 1643).
--
-- The shared s. 3 building blocks this file reads (DogProfile, `the dog is
-- a dangerous dog`, and the Penalty record shape) live in
-- dog-act-domain.l4, imported above. This file constructs its OWN
-- dollar-amount Penalty presets, local to it -- the dog-act-1976.l4
-- presets (e.g. `standard dangerous dog offence penalty`) are declared
-- LOCAL to that file and are not importable, and in any case this Part's
-- own penalty amounts differ from theirs.
--
-- No PARTY/MUST/WITHIN regulative rules appear in this file: every duty in
-- this Part is either open-ended ("on demand", "is to provide a copy") or
-- states no numeric deadline at all, so each is encoded as a BOOLEAN
-- offence/compliance/eligibility predicate rather than forced into a
-- regulative rule against an invented WITHIN. Consequently there is no
-- #TRACE below either -- there is no DEONTIC value in this file to trace.
--
-- SCOPED OUT (cited at their site with @ref, never expanded):
--   * s. 3 "authorised person" / "designated person" -- read by s. 43A(1)
--     and s. 43B(1); defined in s. 3, outside this chunk's assigned range.
--   * s. 16(6) registration certificates -- read by s. 45(2).
--   * s. 33GA(10)-(11) -- read by s. 44(4); the underlying dangerous-dog
--     kill/endanger-life crime is already encoded in dog-act-1976.l4 (as
--     `the crime under s 33GA(11) is committed`), so it is cited here,
--     not re-encoded.
--   * s. 48 and s. 54 (regulation-making powers) -- read by s. 45A(1)(b).
--   * s. 16(3)(a) and s. 33E -- read by s. 45A(6); s. 33E's own
--     declaration-grounds logic is already encoded in dog-act-1976.l4.
--   * Director of Public Prosecutions Act 1991 s. 11 -- read by s. 44(5).
-- =============================================================================

§ `Local penalty presets for this module`

-- The s. 43(1)/(2) shape: dangerous-dog band carries a $500 further-offence
-- fine (Interpretation Act 1984 s. 71, not computed -- see dog-act-domain.l4);
-- the non-dangerous-dog band does not.
`enforcement offence penalty -- dangerous dog (s 43)` MEANS
    Penalty WITH fine IS JUST 10_000, `minimum fine` IS JUST 500, `imprisonment months` IS NOTHING, `further offence fine` IS JUST 500

`enforcement offence penalty -- non-dangerous dog (s 43)` MEANS
    Penalty WITH fine IS JUST 5_000, `minimum fine` IS NOTHING, `imprisonment months` IS NOTHING, `further offence fine` IS NOTHING

§ `Part VII -- Enforcement`
§§ `43. Offences relating to enforcement etc.`

DECLARE `S43(1) Conduct` HAS
    `owner or occupier fails to facilitate an authorised person's (or an assistant's or interpreter's) exercise of duties`                        IS A BOOLEAN  -- (a)
    `impedes, delays or obstructs an authorised person exercising a power or duty under this Act`                                                  IS A BOOLEAN  -- (b)
    `fails without lawful excuse to produce a dog when required, or to allow its examination once produced`                                        IS A BOOLEAN  -- (c)
    `interferes with, releases or removes a seized or detained dog from lawful custody, or damages or destroys the structure detaining it, or attempts to do any of those things` IS A BOOLEAN  -- (d)

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 43(1)
GIVEN conduct IS A `S43(1) Conduct`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 43(1) offence -- obstructing enforcement or interfering with a seized dog` conduct MEANS
        conduct's `owner or occupier fails to facilitate an authorised person's (or an assistant's or interpreter's) exercise of duties`
    OR  conduct's `impedes, delays or obstructs an authorised person exercising a power or duty under this Act`
    OR  conduct's `fails without lawful excuse to produce a dog when required, or to allow its examination once produced`
    OR  conduct's `interferes with, releases or removes a seized or detained dog from lawful custody, or damages or destroys the structure detaining it, or attempts to do any of those things`

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 43(1) Penalty; s. 43(2) Penalty
GIVEN dog IS A DogProfile
GIVETH A Penalty
`s 43 penalty` dog MEANS
    IF `the dog is a dangerous dog` dog
    THEN `enforcement offence penalty -- dangerous dog (s 43)`
    ELSE `enforcement offence penalty -- non-dangerous dog (s 43)`

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 43(2)
GIVEN `person fails without lawful excuse to produce a certificate or other document issued under this Act when required, or fails to allow its examination once produced` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 43(2) offence -- failing to produce or permit examination of a certificate or document` `person fails without lawful excuse to produce a certificate or other document issued under this Act when required, or fails to allow its examination once produced` MEANS
    `person fails without lawful excuse to produce a certificate or other document issued under this Act when required, or fails to allow its examination once produced`

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 43(3)
GIVEN `body corporate may be charged with an offence against this Act` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `person is concerned in or takes part in the management of that body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `person was, by act or omission, directly or indirectly, knowingly concerned in or party to the commission of the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a manager may also be charged with the body corporate's offence under s 43(3)` `body corporate may be charged with an offence against this Act` `person is concerned in or takes part in the management of that body corporate` `person was, by act or omission, directly or indirectly, knowingly concerned in or party to the commission of the offence` MEANS
        `body corporate may be charged with an offence against this Act`
    AND `person is concerned in or takes part in the management of that body corporate`
    AND `person was, by act or omission, directly or indirectly, knowingly concerned in or party to the commission of the offence`

§§ `43AA. False or misleading information`

DECLARE `S43AA Context` HAS
    `in, or in connection with, an application for an approval under this Act`     IS A BOOLEAN  -- (1)(a)
    `in, or in connection with, a notice or document given under this Act`        IS A BOOLEAN  -- (1)(b)
    `in dealing with a person performing a function under this Act`              IS A BOOLEAN  -- (1)(c)
    `in compliance, or purported compliance, with a requirement under this Act`  IS A BOOLEAN  -- (1)(d)

DECLARE `S43AA Conduct` HAS
    `the person knows the statement or information is false or misleading in a material particular` IS A BOOLEAN  -- (2)(a)
    `the statement or information omits anything without which it is, to the person's knowledge, misleading in a material particular` IS A BOOLEAN  -- (2)(b)

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 43AA(1)-(2)
GIVEN context IS A `S43AA Context`, conduct IS A `S43AA Conduct`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 43AA offence -- false or misleading information` context conduct MEANS
    LET `in a covered context` MEANS
            context's `in, or in connection with, an application for an approval under this Act`
        OR  context's `in, or in connection with, a notice or document given under this Act`
        OR  context's `in dealing with a person performing a function under this Act`
        OR  context's `in compliance, or purported compliance, with a requirement under this Act`
    IN
            `in a covered context`
        AND (   conduct's `the person knows the statement or information is false or misleading in a material particular`
             OR conduct's `the statement or information omits anything without which it is, to the person's knowledge, misleading in a material particular`)

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 43AA(1) Penalty
`s 43AA penalty` MEANS
    Penalty WITH fine IS JUST 5_000, `minimum fine` IS NOTHING, `imprisonment months` IS NOTHING, `further offence fine` IS NOTHING

§§ `43A. Name and address to be supplied`

-- "relevant person" (s 43A(1)) is folded into the one fact below per house
-- style: it means an authorised person or a designated person, terms
-- defined in s. 3, outside this chunk's assigned range.
--   @ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 3 "authorised person", "designated person" -- scoped out, fact-supplied
@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 43A(2)
GIVEN `person is alleged by a relevant person (an authorised person or a designated person) to be concerned in the commission of an offence against this Act` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `person gave their full name, date of birth and residential address to that relevant person on demand` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 43A(2) offence -- failing to supply name, date of birth and address on demand` `person is alleged by a relevant person (an authorised person or a designated person) to be concerned in the commission of an offence against this Act` `person gave their full name, date of birth and residential address to that relevant person on demand` MEANS
        `person is alleged by a relevant person (an authorised person or a designated person) to be concerned in the commission of an offence against this Act`
    AND NOT `person gave their full name, date of birth and residential address to that relevant person on demand`

`name and address offence penalty -- dangerous dog (s 43A)` MEANS
    Penalty WITH fine IS JUST 10_000, `minimum fine` IS JUST 500, `imprisonment months` IS NOTHING, `further offence fine` IS NOTHING

`name and address offence penalty -- non-dangerous dog (s 43A)` MEANS
    Penalty WITH fine IS JUST 5_000, `minimum fine` IS NOTHING, `imprisonment months` IS NOTHING, `further offence fine` IS NOTHING

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 43A(2) Penalty
GIVEN dog IS A DogProfile
GIVETH A Penalty
`s 43A(2) penalty` dog MEANS
    IF `the dog is a dangerous dog` dog
    THEN `name and address offence penalty -- dangerous dog (s 43A)`
    ELSE `name and address offence penalty -- non-dangerous dog (s 43A)`

§§ `43B. General powers of relevant persons`

DECLARE `S43B Authorised Purpose Facts` HAS
    `investigating whether, or collecting evidence that, an offence is being or has been committed against this Act` IS A BOOLEAN  -- (1)(a)
    `investigating whether, or collecting evidence that, grounds exist for the cancellation of an approval to breed, a pet shop approval or a dog supply approval` IS A BOOLEAN  -- (1)(b)

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 43B(1)
GIVEN f IS A `S43B Authorised Purpose Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the purpose is an authorised purpose under s 43B(1)` f MEANS
        f's `investigating whether, or collecting evidence that, an offence is being or has been committed against this Act`
    OR  f's `investigating whether, or collecting evidence that, grounds exist for the cancellation of an approval to breed, a pet shop approval or a dog supply approval`

-- s 43B(2)(a)-(e) then lists the specific things a relevant person may do
-- once the gate below is open (examine/seize/copy documents; take
-- photographs/recordings; direct a person to answer questions; examine or
-- scan a dog; take any other reasonably necessary action). That list is an
-- enabling menu with no separate legal consequence of its own -- nothing a
-- downstream rule in this Act would test one item of it against -- so it is
-- left as inert prose; the gate itself is the fact worth encoding.
@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 43B(2)
GIVEN `premises were lawfully entered` IS A BOOLEAN, f IS A `S43B Authorised Purpose Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a relevant person may exercise a s 43B(2) power` `premises were lawfully entered` f MEANS
        `premises were lawfully entered`
    AND `the purpose is an authorised purpose under s 43B(1)` f

§§ `44. Enforcement proceedings`

-- s 44(1) was deleted; noted, not encoded.

DECLARE EnforcementCapacity HAS
    `is a police officer, proceeding in the name of the Crown`                                        IS A BOOLEAN  -- (2)(a)
    `is an employee of a local government, authorised by the local government, proceeding in its name` IS A BOOLEAN  -- (2)(b)
    `is the CEO or a designated person`                                                                 IS A BOOLEAN  -- (2)(ba)
    `is a person aggrieved`                                                                              IS A BOOLEAN  -- (2)(c)

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 44(2)
GIVEN capacity IS AN EnforcementCapacity
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the party may commence enforcement proceedings under s 44(2)` capacity MEANS
        capacity's `is a police officer, proceeding in the name of the Crown`
    OR  capacity's `is an employee of a local government, authorised by the local government, proceeding in its name`
    OR  capacity's `is the CEO or a designated person`
    OR  capacity's `is a person aggrieved`

-- s 44(3): where proceedings are taken by a local government employee, the
-- CEO or a designated person, no proof of authorisation is required -- an
-- averment of authorisation on the process is deemed sufficient proof of
-- that fact. This is a court-procedure deeming rule about how standing is
-- PROVED, not a fact any rule in this file needs to test as an input, so it
-- is left as inert prose rather than a force-fit boolean.

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 44(4)
GIVEN capacity IS AN EnforcementCapacity, `the offence is against s 33GA(10)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the party may commence proceedings for this offence under s 44` capacity `the offence is against s 33GA(10)` MEANS
    IF `the offence is against s 33GA(10)`
    THEN capacity's `is a police officer, proceeding in the name of the Crown`
    ELSE `the party may commence enforcement proceedings under s 44(2)` capacity
        -- @ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 33GA(10)-(11) -- scoped out; already encoded (as s 33GA(11)) in dog-act-1976.l4

-- s 44(5): subsections (2) and (4) do not limit the Director of Public
-- Prosecutions' functions under the Director of Public Prosecutions Act
-- 1991 s. 11 -- inert; a saving clause, no fact for this file to test.
--   @ref Director of Public Prosecutions Act 1991 s. 11 -- scoped out, not expanded

§§ `45. Evidentiary provisions`

-- s 45(1)(a): an averment in a prosecution notice of any of these 14 kinds
-- is, without more, evidence of the fact averred. The deeming is
-- unconditional across the whole closed list -- there is no fact for a
-- downstream rule to test beyond "is this averment one of these kinds",
-- which is exactly what the enum's membership already states. Kept as a
-- DECLAREd enum for structural fidelity to the numbered list; no computed
-- BOOLEAN wraps it, per the "purely administrative machinery" carve-out.
DECLARE `S45(1)(a) Averment` IS ONE OF
    `a specified person was the registered owner of the dog at a particular time`            -- (i)
    `the dog was ordinarily kept by a specified person`                                       -- (ii)
    `a specified person had the dog in the person's possession or under the person's control` -- (iii)
    `a specified person was the occupier of premises where the dog was ordinarily kept or ordinarily permitted to live` -- (iv)
    `the dog was not registered`                                                               -- (v)
    `the dog was of a particular breed or was a mix of particular breeds`                      -- (vi)
    `the dog was not sterilised`                                                                -- (vii)
    `the dog was not microchipped`                                                              -- (viii)
    `a specified person's name was recorded as the owner of the dog in a microchip database`   -- (ix)
    `a specified database was a microchip database`                                            -- (x)
    `a specified person or body was a microchip database company in relation to the dog`       -- (xi)
    `the dog had reached 3 months of age`                                                       -- (xii)
    `the dog was under 3 months of age`                                                         -- (xiii)
    `the dog was the pup of a particular dog`                                                    -- (xiv)

-- s 45(1)(b), (2A): of that same broad territory, only these five facts
-- carry a REVERSE onus -- the person making the assertion must prove it,
-- rather than the general rule that the prosecution proves its case. This
-- is the genuinely testable fact, encoded as a checkbox record (drafting
-- pattern: independent flags + a disjunction, not an enum, because a single
-- assertion is being tested for membership in this narrower list).
DECLARE `S45 Reverse-Onus Facts` HAS
    `assertion is that the dog was registered at a particular time`             IS A BOOLEAN
    `assertion is that the dog was sterilised at a particular time`             IS A BOOLEAN
    `assertion is that the dog was microchipped at a particular time`           IS A BOOLEAN
    `assertion is that the dog was under 3 months of age at a particular time`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `assertion is that the dog was not a dangerous dog (restricted breed)`      IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 45(1)(b), (2A)
GIVEN a IS A `S45 Reverse-Onus Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the onus of proving the asserted fact lies on the person making the assertion` a MEANS
        a's `assertion is that the dog was registered at a particular time`
    OR  a's `assertion is that the dog was sterilised at a particular time`
    OR  a's `assertion is that the dog was microchipped at a particular time`
    OR  a's `assertion is that the dog was under 3 months of age at a particular time`
    OR  a's `assertion is that the dog was not a dangerous dog (restricted breed)`

-- s 45(2): a s. 16(6) registration certificate, or a certified copy of a
-- register entry, is evidence of the matters it sets out without proof of
-- the signature or of the certifying officer's status. Another
-- court-procedure deeming rule about the weight of a document, not a fact
-- any rule in this file needs as an input.
--   @ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 16(6) -- scoped out, not expanded

§§ `45A. Modified penalties`

-- AMBIGUITY (F-S45A1-CAP-SCOPE): does the "$10,000 maximum penalty" cap in
-- (1)(a) also gate (1)(b) ("a regulation made under section 48 or 54")? The
-- text reads "for an offence -- (a) against a provision of this Act, other
-- than the regulations, for which the maximum penalty does not exceed
-- $10,000; or (b) a regulation made under section 48 or 54." (a) and (b) are
-- parallel alternatives, and the cap clause sits grammatically inside (a)
-- alone. Taken: the cap gates (a) only; (b) is an independent, uncapped
-- route at the s 45A(1) stage (s 45A(3)'s separate 10%-of-maximum cap on the
-- MODIFIED PENALTY AMOUNT itself still applies regardless of which route
-- (1) is reached through). Rejected: the cap also gates (b). See
-- sources/fork-register.json entry F-S45A1-CAP-SCOPE. Witnessed below by a
-- $20,000-max-penalty regulation made under s 48/54 still qualifying.
@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 45A(1)
GIVEN `offence is against a provision of this Act, other than the regulations` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `offence is against a regulation made under s 48 or s 54` IS A BOOLEAN,
      penalty IS A Penalty
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`regulations may provide a modified penalty for this offence under s 45A(1)` `offence is against a provision of this Act, other than the regulations` `offence is against a regulation made under s 48 or s 54` penalty MEANS
    LET `maximum penalty is at or below $10,000` MEANS
        CONSIDER penalty's fine
        WHEN JUST `maximum fine` THEN `maximum fine` AT MOST 10_000
        WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    IN
            (`offence is against a provision of this Act, other than the regulations` AND `maximum penalty is at or below $10,000`)
        OR  `offence is against a regulation made under s 48 or s 54`
            -- @ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 48, s. 54 -- scoped out, fact-supplied

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 45A(2)
GIVEN `offence is against a local law` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`local laws may provide a modified penalty for this offence under s 45A(2)` `offence is against a local law` MEANS
    `offence is against a local law`

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 45A(3)
GIVEN `modified penalty amount` IS A NUMBER, penalty IS A Penalty
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the modified penalty complies with the s 45A(3) cap` `modified penalty amount` penalty MEANS
    CONSIDER penalty's fine
    WHEN JUST `maximum fine` THEN `modified penalty amount` AT MOST (`maximum fine` TIMES (10%))
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 45A(4)
GIVEN `person does not contest an allegation that they committed an offence to which a modified penalty applies` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `an acknowledgment was produced that the modified penalty has been paid to the local government or designated person who gave the notice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a s 45A(4) defence is established` `person does not contest an allegation that they committed an offence to which a modified penalty applies` `an acknowledgment was produced that the modified penalty has been paid to the local government or designated person who gave the notice` MEANS
        `person does not contest an allegation that they committed an offence to which a modified penalty applies`
    AND `an acknowledgment was produced that the modified penalty has been paid to the local government or designated person who gave the notice`

-- s 45A(5): payment of a modified penalty is not an admission of liability
-- and does not affect a civil claim -- inert; a legal-effect statement, not
-- a fact any rule in this file tests.
-- s 45A(6): for the purposes of s. 16(3)(a) and s. 33E, payment of a
-- modified penalty may be taken as evidence of the dog's behaviour -- both
-- cross-referenced provisions are outside this chunk (s. 33E's grounds
-- logic is already encoded in dog-act-1976.l4), so this is cited, not
-- expanded, rather than re-modelled here.
--   @ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 16(3)(a), s. 33E -- scoped out, not expanded

§§ `46A. Order to attend dog training course, ban on owning or keeping dogs`

-- s 46A(1)(a)/(b) are jointly exhaustive -- every offence either carries a
-- minimum penalty (Penalty's `minimum fine` is JUST something) or does not
-- -- so the court's power to order a training course is available on ANY
-- conviction; what differs is only whether the order is IN ADDITION to the
-- penalty, or an ALTERNATIVE TO (as well as in addition to) it. That
-- distinction is the fact worth carrying, via the "Total enum over MAYBE"
-- pattern: MAYBE because "not convicted" is a genuine absence of any basis.
DECLARE `Training Course Order Basis` IS ONE OF
    `in addition to the penalty (the offence carries a minimum penalty, s 46A(1)(a))`
    `as an alternative to, or in addition to, the penalty (any other offence, s 46A(1)(b))`

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 46A(1)
GIVEN `person was convicted of an offence against this Act` IS A BOOLEAN, penalty IS A Penalty
GIVETH A MAYBE `Training Course Order Basis`
`the basis on which the court may order a dog training course under s 46A(1)` `person was convicted of an offence against this Act` penalty MEANS
    IF NOT `person was convicted of an offence against this Act`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE
        CONSIDER penalty's `minimum fine`
        WHEN JUST m    THEN JUST `in addition to the penalty (the offence carries a minimum penalty, s 46A(1)(a))`
        WHEN NOTHING   THEN JUST `as an alternative to, or in addition to, the penalty (any other offence, s 46A(1)(b))`

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 46A(2)
GIVEN `person was convicted of an offence against this Act` IS A BOOLEAN, `number of the person's previous convictions for offences against this Act` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the court may make a s 46A(2) banning order` `person was convicted of an offence against this Act` `number of the person's previous convictions for offences against this Act` MEANS
        `person was convicted of an offence against this Act`
    AND `number of the person's previous convictions for offences against this Act` AT LEAST 2

DECLARE `S46A3 Notice` HAS
    `copy of the order given to the local government for the district in which the person lives` IS A BOOLEAN
    `copy of the order given to the CEO`                                                          IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 46A(3)
GIVEN n IS A `S46A3 Notice`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the court has complied with the s 46A(3) notice requirement` n MEANS
        n's `copy of the order given to the local government for the district in which the person lives`
    AND n's `copy of the order given to the CEO`

@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 46A(4)
GIVEN `an order was made against the person under s 46A(1) or (2)` IS A BOOLEAN, `person complied with that order` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 46A(4) offence -- contempt of court for non-compliance with a training or banning order` `an order was made against the person under s 46A(1) or (2)` `person complied with that order` MEANS
        `an order was made against the person under s 46A(1) or (2)`
    AND NOT `person complied with that order`

-- s 46A(5): maxima -- imprisonment not exceeding 12 months, or a fine not
-- exceeding $5,000, or both. Represented with the maxima in Penalty's
-- fine/imprisonment-months fields, as elsewhere in this corpus.
@ref Dog Act 1976 (WA) s. 46A(5) Penalty
`s 46A(5) penalty -- contempt of court (maxima)` MEANS
    Penalty WITH fine IS JUST 5_000, `minimum fine` IS NOTHING, `imprisonment months` IS JUST 12, `further offence fine` IS NOTHING
-- s 46A(5) further provides that, in default of immediate payment of the
-- fine, the person may instead be imprisoned until the fine is paid or for
-- 12 months, whichever is shorter -- a default-imprisonment mechanism that
-- has no field of its own in the Penalty record (fine / minimum fine /
-- imprisonment months / further offence fine) and is recorded here as
-- inert prose rather than forced into that shape.

